-

Comes with files Cooling_Lab_Starter.xlsx and Cooling_Lab_40_Minutes.md


The temperature always decreases by x/5 - 6 only for values above room temperature 
If the container starts at exactly room temperature, its temperature should stay the same because there is no temperature difference between the container and the room. 


It means it closes 30% of the gap between its temperature and the room temperature.

Prediction A predicts a temperature below room temperature, which is not possible.
Model B predicts that cooling depends on the temperature difference between the container and the room. It does not predict cooling when both temperatures are equal, so I choose B.


The container will cool faster with the fan switched on because moving air increases heat transfer from the container.
However, neither model will change its prediction because neither model includes air movement as an input.
The fan caused an extra temperature drop of 6°C. (41-35)

I would choose Test 1 with the fan on because the two ideas give different predictions.
31-6=25*C
35-20=15 ; 15x0.2=3 ; 31-3 =28*C
Keep the room temperature at 20°C, the starting temperature at 35°C, the same time and use the same container and blanket.
If the final temperature is close to 25°C, it would support Idea 1. If it is closer to 28°C, it would support Idea 2.


Initially, my hypothesis thought the container would always lose the same number of degrees after ten minutes.
Model A predicted a 9°C drop, but Model B predicted that the container would lose 30% of the temperature difference between it and the room.
Model B was more accurate because its average error was only 0.50°C, compared with 5.50°C for Model A. It also made more sense because the container shouldn't cool below room temperature.
However, Model B did not apply for the fan test.
For my next experiment, I would try Test 1 again with the fan switched on. One idea predicts 25°C and the other predicts 28°C. I would keep everything else the same so it is a fair test.
